WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.020
Film Courage: Where do you struggle most with
writing screenplays?

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:05.330
R.L. Scott, Writer/Director: Interestingly
enough it’s just writing.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:12.360
I think I’ve been writing for so long where
I’m at the point now where I wish there

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:21.630
was some really good software where I could
just talk and it would just write it in script

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.960
format for me you know.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:33.630
The physical act of getting it out of your
head I think is the hardest part now because

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:42.080
it’s usually most creatives especially a
few storytellers there’s never a time when

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:50.350
you don’t have stories just constantly coming
into your head like ideas.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:59.250
Even if you flush out the idea fully, it’s
just the physical act of typing.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:07.360
I think that’s the thing when I was really
young it was fun you know just young and typing

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:14.390
and everything is great you can just type
for hours and hours everyday because I do

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:26.270
so many other things I think now I would just
really have that be automated.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:35.920
I’m getting to a place now where I have
a few younger writers that I’m working with

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.910
where I can sort of outline the story not
for everything.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:46.790
There are certain films where I’m like this
is just my baby right here but certain projects

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:51.100
where I’ll do an outline and then I’ll
send it to them and then they can write the

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.770
script based on my outline and then send it
back and I’ll just like punch it up.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:01.610
So that’s been pretty cool.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:07.100
I’m waiting on that magic thing where I
don’t have to type anymore.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.220
Film Courage: What about writing in long form?

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.510
Is this appealing to you or no?

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:25.200
R.L.: When I started out it was on an old
school typewriter with the big keys that come

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.280
out…you know…[makes typing sounds].

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.590
That’s how I started out.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.920
And then moved to the electric and then the
computer.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:48.230
But I want to talk and have something typed
for me.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.750
If I could never physically type another script
again I’d be great.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:54.420
That would be awesome.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.640
Film Courage: Is this because you don’t
like the screenplay format?

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.570
You’d almost rather do it in novel form?

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:09.700
R.L.: I think it’s just the physical act
because it takes so much brainpower to write.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.400
It’s very exhausting.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:20.240
It takes your soul.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.980
I think it’s just that part of it.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:29.120
I love telling stories and I still have a
lot of things I want to say but I think because

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:37.300
I still edit, I still shoot, I still direct,
I still do sound design, certain products,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:50.720
because I’m doing all of that other stuff
when I physically have to type a script, I

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:59.319
might get a couple pages and I’m done.

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:09.629
Unless you are so in it and you’re to get
it out of your head, that’s different but

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:12.160
I try to set goals.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:18.379
Because I’m older now and it’s harder
to write as much as I did before so I set

00:04:18.379 --> 00:04:25.279
goals such as a certain amount of pages or
that sort of thing.

00:04:25.279 --> 00:04:32.259
I try to do it where I break it into sections.

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:38.589
Right now I’m finishing up the ALMIGHTY
STREET TEAM, adapting those comic books into

00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:43.280
a script and I’ve probably got 30 pages
left.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.789
Like I’m almost there.

00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:55.279
And that’s been difficult too adapting something
that already exists, a comic book or doing

00:04:55.279 --> 00:05:01.960
an adaptation, it’s very different than
when you are coming up with something completely

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.180
original.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:12.610
It’s riding that line of how much do you
keep, how much do you change, bringing it

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:19.860
from the comic book into live-action there
are certain things that are going to operate

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:20.860
differently.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.840
That’s been a unique challenge.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:35.069
This is the first time I’ve done that, adapted
anything that was pre-existing,

00:05:35.069 --> 00:05:48.530
a unique challenge but very different because
I want to stay true to the spirit of the material

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:53.979
and there are great moments in the material
that you definitely want in there and then

00:05:53.979 --> 00:05:58.780
certain places you have to sort of expand
it to make it work.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:03.800
But I think I’ve found a really good space
with it.

